Qore optional TypeScript catalogue source package
================================================

This recipe builds the full catalogue, with its production JavaScript dependency
tree, as a separate Architecture: all package. The native V8 source package
continues to exclude ts/. The catalogue requires the V8 package containing the
immutable directory-map fix, structured optional-resource initialization errors,
and matching HubSpot presentation catalogs. Its versioned compatibility
fingerprints reject unreviewed schema changes before cache activation. Activate
it explicitly as described in README.Debian.

PUBLICATION GATE: the full catalogue has not been cleared for redistribution.
The schema-provenance.json file records missing/conflicting grants, for the remaining bundled schemas. HubSpot documents are excluded entirely;
see README.Debian for the explicit per-user cache. The Facebook SDK's Platform
License also needs a PPA eligibility review. Complete the per-file Debian
copyright mapping and preserve all applicable terms before uploading a source
or binary package to any public repository. These checks apply to testing PPAs
as well as general releases. No package upload is performed by this recipe.

Build from the .dsc and both upstream components: the first-party ts/ sources
plus the versioned orig-vendor tarball. The vendor component supplies Yarn
4.12.0, its license and the exact cache archives listed in vendor-manifest.json.
Do not substitute an existing node_modules directory, a different Yarn release
or a newly resolved dependency graph. The manifest validates package.json,
yarn.lock, the Yarn executable and every cache archive before and after build.

For a checkout, obtain the matching vendor component from the qualified source
package and extract it at ts/vendor/. This generated directory is not committed
to Git. Dependency updates require a newly prefetched and reviewed component,
updated integrity records and the same offline qualification. The Yarn download
is https://repo.yarnpkg.com/4.12.0/packages/yarnpkg-cli/bin/yarn.js; its expected
SHA-256 is in vendor-manifest.json and its packageManager SHA-512 is in package.json.
All network prefetching happens before creating the source package. Upstream npm
archives and their license/notice files are preserved in the vendor component.

Install declared Build-Depends, then run dpkg-buildpackage -b -us -uc from the
extracted source directory. Use a clean Ubuntu 26.04 amd64 build environment;
other build architectures and distributions have not been qualified. npm's
optional development bindings are platform dependent even though the installed
JavaScript package is architecture independent. No native executable or addon
is permitted in the installed catalogue tree.

The build disables Yarn network access, global cache reuse, telemetry and
third-party lifecycle scripts. Its cache/global data remain in the build tree,
so an inaccessible home directory is supported. It compiles the first-party
TypeScript and prunes schemas using the project's normal build, runs offline
Jest regressions and creates a production-only dependency tree. The map is
generated with Qore's own YAML serializer for byte-identical read-only loading.

The runtime tree excludes Rollup's unused native build binding, node-gyp added
by Yarn for evp_bytestokey's test:prepare script, KaTeX font-generation tools,
identified upstream test/release helpers and development/VCS metadata. It keeps
runtime code, generated fonts and upstream notices. KaTeX rendering, the JS
EVP helper, a mocked Contentful request and a real private CLI invocation test
these choices. CLI scripts are executable; type declarations and other data
remain non-executable. Private embedded libraries/fonts retain their upstream
relative layout; their maintenance and security updates remain this package's
responsibility.

Build tests can be disabled with the usual nocheck option/profile. The installed
runtime autopkgtest checks production-only dependencies and the exact qualified
app/action inventory in fresh Qore tiered and AST processes, using installed
modules and an unprivileged test user. Live service API operations require their
own credentials and are not covered by these offline tests. Supplement with
repeat-build comparison, source/payload parity, package inspection, Lintian,
HubSpot's native bridge regression and install/purge/reinstall qualification.
